67 documents marked as confidential 92  marked secret 25 marked top secret these  are what the FBI said were inside 15  boxes retrieved by the national archives  in January from former president Donald  Trump's Mar-A-Lago home they were handed  over after linked the efforts to secure  Trump's presidential records and that's  when they said oh my gosh this is a lot  bigger than just a presidential records  act violation this could be a classified  information National Security risk and  so we need to bring the justice  department in on this it triggered an  FBI search of the Florida property in  August where the justice department says  even more highly classified documents  were found the standards around  classification and declassification are  key to the federal investigation into  Trump and his defense here's how that  process works including what Authority  the president has and doesn't have  the Constitution gives the president as  commander-in-chief authority to decide  what should be classified and recent  presidents have managed this by writing  an Executive Order Detailing the rules  around the classification and  declassification process it's  essentially been the same for decades  Bush really just updated Clinton's order  Obama just updated bushes and that's  still the current procedure neither  Trump nor Biden released new ones the  order gives the authority to decide what  should be classified to people called  Original classifiers they're folks like  the director of intelligence and other  higher-ups the executive branch which  includes the military employs more than  4 million people less than 1500 of them  have this important designation  they write out classification guides  deciding what information should be  classified and to what level that guide  would be distributed to other members of  the agency so they would know to label  documents with the right classification  and know whom they could and couldn't  distribute it to  confidential is the lowest  classification level information that  could cause damage to National Security  the next level is secret then top secret  there's also additional classifications  like special access programs that only  limited people can view the bin Laden  raid for example would have been a  special Access program there's also  sensitive compartmented information  which contains details about intelligent  sources and methods basically  information from spies they can only be  used and stored in special secured  facilities or Skiffs the same goes with  special access program files  both these kinds of Highly classified  materials were found in documents from  Mar-A-Lago  when marking a document containing  classified information the author must  include which original classifier has  deemed it classified and why and  directions on how it should be  Declassified it can be automatic after a  certain date or after an event takes  place but it generally can't be more  than 25 years from its creation  according to the latest executive order  if information is to be Declassified  earlier like what Trump says he did that  Authority lies with the same people who  could classify it in the first place the  original classifiers and the president  and the procedure is similar typically a  declassification guide would be written  and sent out the classified documents  would then also be changed to show that  the information was Declassified and  it's important to note that this  Authority resides in the position not  the individual who holds that position  so if an original classifier retires  that person no longer has the authority  to classify or declassify but whoever  takes their position does this includes  the president  the President also doesn't have  authority over all classified  information like nuclear weapons are  classified by law and any information  shared by other countries can only be  Declassified by them  here's where it gets a little  complicated in regard to Trump 